---
/**
 * Every B2B capability GoCommerce has today, each naming the file it lives
 * in — and the edge named too, because a buyer who finds a missing feature
 * after choosing the engine was misled, however good the rest of the page.
 * Pricing is core's; companies, terms, approvals and quotes are the b2b
 * module's; buyers are the identity module's accounts.
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro'

const path = '/solutions/b2b/'
const abs = (p: string) => new URL(p, Astro.site!).href

const graph = [
  { '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': abs(path + '#page'), url: abs(path), name: 'B2B commerce on GoCommerce', isPartOf: { '@id': abs('/#site') }, about: { '@id': abs('/gocommerce/#app') }, inLanguage: 'en' },
]

const faq = [
  { q: 'How does a business customer get its own prices?', a: 'A company points at a customer group, and its buyers’ addresses are in that group. A price list attached to the group holds rows of variant, minimum quantity and amount, so a buyer’s cart is priced from the list at the quantity they order — with priority, a date window and a channel when two lists could apply.' },
  { q: 'Can somebody get a dealer’s prices by typing the dealer’s email?', a: 'No. A group price reaches a cart only through an address something has proven: a signed-in account whose email is confirmed, an operator placing the order, or a storefront backend vouching for its own signed-in customer. The email typed onto a cart is a contact address and earns nothing. Buyers join a company by an invitation mailed to their address, and accepting it is the proof.' },
  { q: 'How do payment terms and credit limits work?', a: 'A company with an account pays on account: the order is confirmed with payment pending and a due date its net days out, and settling the invoice marks it paid. Its credit limit is checked inside the checkout, against the order’s final total, before the order exists — under a lock per company, so two buyers ordering at the same moment cannot both fit under a limit only one of them fits under.' },
  { q: 'How do approvals work?', a: 'A company sets an approval threshold. A buyer whose order is over it gets an approval request instead of an order, and the company’s approvers are emailed. An approver places it at the prices it was submitted at, or rejects it with a reason; the buyer can withdraw it. Admins and approvers order without approval.' },
  { q: 'How do quotes work?', a: 'A buyer asks for a quote on quantities. The store prices each line, adds a reply and an expiry, and sends it. The buyer accepts and the order is placed at exactly those prices — over the catalogue and over the company’s own price list — or it waits for approval when it is over the buyer’s limit. A quote becomes one order at most.' },
  { q: 'Can a business get a statement of its account?', a: 'Yes. Pick a period and you get what was owed at the start, every order and payment, what is owed at the end, and how late it is in 30-day steps. The store sees it in the admin, a company’s own admins through the API, and both can download it as a spreadsheet. A payment is dated when the store recorded it; if that record is gone, the line says so instead of guessing.' },
  { q: 'Can I limit what a company can buy?', a: 'Yes. A catalogue is a set of categories, each with everything under it, plus single products. Hold a company to one and checkout refuses anything outside it, however it got into the basket. Quick orders leave those lines out and say why, quotes cannot include them, and the buyer can list what they may buy at their own prices.' },
  { q: 'Can buyers order from a spreadsheet?', a: 'Yes. Upload a CSV with a SKU or variant id and a quantity on each row, up to 500 rows. What can go in goes in; anything that cannot is listed with its row number and the reason.' },
  { q: 'Can a buyer’s procurement system order without a person logging in?', a: 'Yes. An API key carries a role’s rights, a prefix that identifies it in a log line, its last-used time and a revocation. Checkout takes an Idempotency-Key, so a system that retries a timed-out request does not place the order twice.' },
  { q: 'Can consumer enquiries go to the nearest dealer?', a: 'Yes. A dealer is a company with territories — a country, a state or a postcode prefix — and an enquiry posted from the storefront goes to the dealer whose territory fits it most specifically, with its admins emailed. The public answer never names the dealer, so the form cannot be used to map the network, and the store sees and reassigns every lead, including the ones no territory covers.' },
  { q: 'Can each dealer have a store of its own?', a: 'Yes, from one install. Platform mode serves many stores from one process and one PostgreSQL database, each in a schema of its own with its own operators, admin panel and domain, created and suspended through a platform API whose credentials open no store.' },
  { q: 'Is it free to use for B2B?', a: 'Yes. GoCommerce is MIT-licensed, and the B2B module is in the same repository as the engine: no paid edition, no B2B add-on.' },
]
---
<Base
  title="B2B Commerce on GoCommerce — Accounts, Credit, Quotes"
  description="Open-source B2B commerce: company accounts, credit limits and statements, approvals, quotes, per-company catalogues, spreadsheet orders and dealer lead routing."
  path={path}
  ogTitle="B2B commerce on GoCommerce"
  ogDescription="Company accounts, buyer roles, credit limits and net terms, approvals, quotes, purchase orders and tiered pricing — MIT, with the edges named."
  nav="solutions"
  crumbs={[{ name: 'Solutions', href: '/solutions/' }, { name: 'B2B', href: path }]}
  graph={graph}
  faq={faq}
>
  <section class="wrap page-head">
    <p class="kicker">B2B commerce</p>
    <h1>Sell to businesses on their terms, not just their prices</h1>
    <p class="lede">Companies with buyers in roles, orders on account against a credit limit, approval before a junior buyer spends the company’s money, quotes priced for one buyer, bulk and repeat orders, enquiries routed to the right dealer, and the tiered pricing underneath — in the engine and its MIT-licensed modules, enforced before an order exists rather than cleaned up after.</p>
    <ul class="head-facts">
      <li><b>ext/b2b</b> companies, terms, approvals, quotes</li>
      <li><b>core/pricing.go</b> groups, price lists, quantity breaks</li>
      <li><b>ext/identity</b> buyer accounts, confirmed addresses</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="new" class="section wrap" aria-labelledby="new-title">
    <h2 id="new-title">New in GoCommerce 1.1</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">Five things suppliers asked for, in plain terms.</p>
    <div class="tbl-scroll">
      <table class="reltable fit">
        <caption>What each one does for you</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">New</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Statements</th><td>One page per period: what was owed at the start, every order and payment, what is owed at the end. Download it as a spreadsheet.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">How late it is</th><td>Unpaid money split into not yet due, 1–30, 31–60, 61–90 and over 90 days late.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Terms history</th><td>Every change to a credit limit, payment days or rules, with who changed it and when.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Spreadsheet orders</th><td>Upload a CSV of SKUs and quantities, up to 500 rows. Problem rows come back with their row number.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Catalogues</th><td>Limit a company to some categories and products. Checkout, quick orders and quotes all keep to it.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="everything" class="section wrap" aria-labelledby="everything-title">
    <h2 id="everything-title">Everything B2B in GoCommerce today</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">Grouped by what a business customer meets. Each item is in the code now.</p>
    <div class="featlist">
      <div class="featlist-group">
        <h3>Company accounts</h3>
        <ul class="tick">
          <li><b>Companies</b> with a code, tax number, notes and a status: active, on hold, or closed.</li>
          <li><b>Buyer roles</b>: admin, approver and buyer, the company’s own business rather than the store’s.</li>
          <li><b>Invitations</b> mailed to an address; accepting confirms it and joins the company.</li>
          <li><b>Self-service</b>: a company’s admins add, re-role and remove their own buyers through the storefront API.</li>
          <li><b>Catalogues</b>: hold a company to some categories and products; checkout refuses the rest.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="featlist-group">
        <h3>Pricing</h3>
        <ul class="tick">
          <li><b>Customer groups</b> and <b>price lists</b>, with priority, a date window and a channel.</li>
          <li><b>Quantity breaks</b>: the highest break at or below the quantity ordered, per line.</li>
          <li><b>Proven buyers only</b>: a group price reaches a cart through a confirmed address, never a typed one.</li>
          <li><b>Agreed line prices</b> on quotes and phone orders, over every list.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="featlist-group">
        <h3>Terms and credit</h3>
        <ul class="tick">
          <li><b>Pay on account</b>: confirmed at once, payment due net days later.</li>
          <li><b>Credit limits</b> checked with the order’s final total before it exists.</li>
          <li><b>On hold</b> stops ordering on account; <b>closed</b> stops ordering at all.</li>
          <li><b>Receivables</b>: outstanding, available and overdue per company, and every unpaid order on account.</li>
          <li><b>Statements</b> for any period, with how late each amount is, as a page or a spreadsheet.</li>
          <li><b>Terms history</b>: who changed a credit limit or payment terms, and when.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="featlist-group">
        <h3>Approvals</h3>
        <ul class="tick">
          <li><b>A threshold per company</b>: a buyer’s order over it waits for an approver.</li>
          <li><b>Approvers emailed</b>, and the buyer told the decision.</li>
          <li><b>Placed as submitted</b>: at the prices the approver was shown, whatever the cart does next.</li>
          <li><b>Reject with a reason</b>, or withdraw your own request.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="featlist-group">
        <h3>Quotes</h3>
        <ul class="tick">
          <li><b>Requests for quote</b> on quantities, with a note.</li>
          <li><b>Priced per line</b> by the store, with a reply and an expiry, then sent.</li>
          <li><b>Accepted into an order</b> at the quoted prices — or into an approval.</li>
          <li><b>One order per quote</b>, even when two people accept at once.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="featlist-group">
        <h3>Ordering</h3>
        <ul class="tick">
          <li><b>Bulk order</b>: paste many SKUs or upload a spreadsheet; the lines that cannot go are listed by row, not fatal.</li>
          <li><b>Repeat order</b>: a past order back in the basket, at today’s prices.</li>
          <li><b>Partial checkout</b>: order the lines you choose and keep the rest in the basket.</li>
          <li><b>Purchase order numbers</b> on every order, required per company if you choose.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="featlist-group">
        <h3>Dealer networks</h3>
        <ul class="tick">
          <li><b>Territories</b>: a country, a state or a postcode prefix, each held by one dealer.</li>
          <li><b>Lead routing</b>: a consumer’s enquiry goes to the dealer whose territory fits best.</li>
          <li><b>Dealers see their own leads</b> and mark them contacted, won or lost.</li>
          <li><b>The store sees every lead</b>, catches the unrouted ones and reassigns.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="featlist-group">
        <h3>Many stores</h3>
        <ul class="tick">
          <li><b>A store per dealer or brand</b> from one install and one database.</li>
          <li><b>Each in its own schema</b>, with its own operators, admin panel and domain.</li>
          <li><b>A platform API</b> to create, suspend and delete stores, with its own credentials.</li>
          <li><b>Custom domains</b>, with certificates issued on demand by your proxy.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="featlist-group">
        <h3>Systems</h3>
        <ul class="tick">
          <li><b>API keys</b> with a role, for a procurement system that never logs in.</li>
          <li><b>Idempotent checkout</b>: a retried order is the same order, never a second.</li>
          <li><b>Webhooks</b> out, and <b>numbered invoices</b>.</li>
          <li><b>Admin screens</b> for companies, catalogues, statements, quotes, approvals and receivables.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="order" class="section wrap feature" aria-labelledby="order-title">
    <div class="feature-copy">
      <h2 id="order-title">One buyer’s order, end to end</h2>
      <ul class="tick">
        <li><b>The cart is priced as the buyer</b> — the company’s group prices — once the buyer is signed in with a confirmed address.</li>
        <li><b>Checkout goes through the company</b>: a company-priced basket sent to the public checkout is refused, so nobody routes around approval.</li>
        <li><b>The company’s rules run inside the checkout</b>, with the final total: status, PO number, approval threshold, credit limit.</li>
        <li><b>Refused means nothing happened</b>: no order, no stock reserved, no order number drawn.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="sol-prim">POST /x/b2b/checkout → 201 order · 202 approval · 403 credit_limit_exceeded</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="feature-visual flow" aria-labelledby="fo-cap">
      <figcaption id="fo-cap" class="flow-cap">A buyer checks out on account</figcaption>
      <div class="arch-row"><h3>Buyer</h3><p>role: buyer · address confirmed</p></div>
      <p class="arch-wire"><span>cart priced at the company’s group prices</span></p>
      <div class="arch-row arch-engine"><h3>Checkout guard</h3><p>status · PO · threshold · credit, one company at a time</p></div>
      <p class="arch-wire"><span>under the threshold and the limit</span></p>
      <div class="arch-row arch-store"><h3>Order on account</h3><p>confirmed · payment pending · due in 30 days</p></div>
      <p class="arch-wire"><span>invoice settled</span></p>
      <div class="arch-row arch-data"><h3>Marked paid</h3><p>the credit is free again</p></div>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="controls" class="section wrap" aria-labelledby="controls-title">
    <h2 id="controls-title">Controls, not apologies</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">A limit that is checked after the order exists is a cancellation email. These run before.</p>
    <ul class="evidence">
      <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg><b>A checkout guard</b><span>A module’s rules run inside the checkout transaction with the final figures; a refusal rolls everything back.</span></li>
      <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-coin"/></svg><b>One decision per company</b><span>Credit is checked under a lock per company, so two simultaneous orders cannot overrun the limit together.</span></li>
      <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-users"/></svg><b>Proven addresses</b><span>Group prices follow a confirmed address. A buyer who changes theirs leaves the company’s prices until the new one is confirmed.</span></li>
      <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><b>Snapshots for approval</b><span>An approver approves exactly what was submitted; editing the cart afterwards changes nothing.</span></li>
      <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-tag"/></svg><b>Agreed prices stay agreed</b><span>A quoted price holds over every list and break, on an order no shopper’s token can reach.</span></li>
      <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-braces"/></svg><b>Rights of their own</b><span>Seeing and managing companies, and pricing quotes, are separate rights on the Roles grid.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="edge" class="section wrap" aria-labelledby="edge-title">
    <h2 id="edge-title">Where the edge is</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">What a business buyer may expect that is not there yet.</p>
    <div class="tri">
      <div class="tri-item"><h3>No shipping or tax per company</h3><p>A company gets its own prices and its own catalogue, but pays the store’s shipping rates and tax like everyone else. The public storefront still shows every product; checkout is where a catalogue is enforced.</p></div>
      <div class="tri-item"><h3>One approval threshold</h3><p>A company has one limit above which a buyer waits. Multi-level chains and per-buyer budgets are not modelled.</p></div>
      <div class="tri-item"><h3>The storefront is yours to build</h3><p>The buyer side is an API. Svelte Commerce does not ship company, approval or quote screens yet, and an invoice is issued when an order is paid, not when it ships.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Faq items={faq} title="Questions about B2B" />

  <section class="closing wrap">
    <h2>Create a company, set its terms, invite a buyer.</h2>
    <p>Run the stack with the b2b module, open Customers › Companies in the admin, and place an order on account from the API docs.</p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href="/#one-command" data-ev="quickstart_click">Deploy in minutes</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/solutions/multi-store/">Per-channel pricing</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/solutions/marketplace/">Marketplace</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/solutions/">All solutions</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
